Visible repair challenges aesthetic conventions about perfection and replacement by celebrating evidence of care, maintenance, and longevity visible on product surfaces. Strategies like Japanese kintsugi pottery, visible stitching in clothing, and worn finishes on tools communicate authenticity and stories of use rather than signaling depreciation. Products designed for visible repair acceptance transform cosmetic damage from stigma into character, enabling users to feel pride in maintained possessions rather than shame requiring replacement. This design philosophy directly contradicts planned obsolescence and supports extended product lifespans.